1
00:01:09,770 --> 00:01:13,316
<i>♪ Advice to the lovelorn ♪</i>

2
00:01:13,340 --> 00:01:15,218
<i>♪ To the love torn ♪</i>

3
00:01:15,242 --> 00:01:17,654
<i>♪ To the love worn ♪</i>

4
00:01:17,678 --> 00:01:21,246
<i>♪ Please, listen to me ♪</i>

5
00:01:24,084 --> 00:01:27,697
<i>♪ Advice to the lovers ♪</i>

6
00:01:27,721 --> 00:01:31,134
<i>♪ Who are trying to recover ♪</i>

7
00:01:31,158 --> 00:01:38,096
<i>♪ Some ecstasy ♪</i>

8
00:01:39,266 --> 00:01:42,145
<i>♪ Advice to the man ♪</i>

9
00:01:42,169 --> 00:01:44,113
<i>♪ And the woman ♪</i>

10
00:01:44,137 --> 00:01:45,548
<i>♪ Who are looking ♪</i>

11
00:01:45,572 --> 00:01:49,808
<i>♪ To play their part ♪</i>

12
00:01:53,247 --> 00:01:57,894
<i>♪ Advice to the lovelorn ♪</i>

13
00:01:57,918 --> 00:02:00,230
<i>♪ Be careful when you give ♪</i>

14
00:02:00,254 --> 00:02:04,789
<i>♪ Your heart ♪</i>

15
00:02:08,329 --> 00:02:11,341
Hi. This is Sylvia Richland
with<i> Eye on Atlanta.</i>

16
00:02:11,365 --> 00:02:14,177
Today we're at the spacious
home of Marjorie Manners.

17
00:02:14,201 --> 00:02:16,179
Marjorie is this
year's recipient

18
00:02:16,203 --> 00:02:18,881
of Atlanta's coveted
Golden Peach Award,

19
00:02:18,905 --> 00:02:21,484
given by the local chapter
of the Women of America,

20
00:02:21,508 --> 00:02:24,554
to that person who best
represents family values.

21
00:02:24,578 --> 00:02:26,489
And what better person
to receive this award

22
00:02:26,513 --> 00:02:29,192
than one of America's
favorite columnists.

23
00:02:29,216 --> 00:02:32,796
Marjorie Manners'
column, "Dear Marjorie,"

24
00:02:32,820 --> 00:02:36,399
appears daily in over
300 papers coast to coast.

25
00:02:36,423 --> 00:02:38,968
Atlanta's own Marjorie
has been dispensing advice

26
00:02:38,992 --> 00:02:41,671
to the lovelorn
for over 15 years,

27
00:02:41,695 --> 00:02:45,008
and now we're gonna
meet her family, in person.

28
00:02:45,032 --> 00:02:46,276
Was that too awkward?

29
00:02:46,300 --> 00:02:49,067
No. Do you want
me to do it again? No.

30
00:02:54,274 --> 00:02:55,518
Hello.

31
00:02:55,542 --> 00:02:57,253
Hi, I'm Sylvia Richland
with<i> Eye on Atlanta.</i>

32
00:02:57,277 --> 00:02:59,522
You must be
Marjorie's husband, Lee.

33
00:02:59,546 --> 00:03:01,390
Uh, no. I'm her
brother-in-law, Carter Weston.

34
00:03:01,414 --> 00:03:02,659
Lee's inside. Please come on in.

35
00:03:02,683 --> 00:03:03,782
Oh.

36
00:03:04,852 --> 00:03:06,129
This is my wife, Carol.

37
00:03:06,153 --> 00:03:07,998
Oh, you must be
Marjorie's sister.

38
00:03:08,022 --> 00:03:08,999
That's right.

39
00:03:09,023 --> 00:03:10,700
Oh. That's wonderful.

40
00:03:10,724 --> 00:03:11,701
Ah, please.

41
00:03:11,725 --> 00:03:13,169
Thank you.

42
00:03:13,193 --> 00:03:16,706
Oh, now, you have
to be Lee Manners,

43
00:03:16,730 --> 00:03:17,707
Marjorie's husband.

44
00:03:17,731 --> 00:03:19,108
Ah, that I am. Yes.

45
00:03:19,132 --> 00:03:20,810
Great. And you must be her son?

46
00:03:20,834 --> 00:03:22,979
Uh, no, no. I'm her copy boy.

47
00:03:23,003 --> 00:03:24,180
Oh.

48
00:03:24,204 --> 00:03:26,684
Maybe we should set
up for Marjorie now.

49
00:03:27,374 --> 00:03:29,785
I'm just so overwhelmed.

50
00:03:29,809 --> 00:03:33,289
It's such an unexpected honor.

51
00:03:33,313 --> 00:03:35,791
Really helping people
solve their problems

52
00:03:35,815 --> 00:03:38,328
is rewarding in and of itself.

53
00:03:38,352 --> 00:03:40,696
This makes me
feel almost guilty.

54
00:03:40,720 --> 00:03:43,800
Marjorie, people
across the country say

55
00:03:43,824 --> 00:03:45,669
they don't feel as though
they've read the paper

56
00:03:45,693 --> 00:03:47,236
unless they've read your column.

57
00:03:47,260 --> 00:03:49,773
How does it feel to know
you touch so many lives?

58
00:03:49,797 --> 00:03:53,008
All I am to my readers
is a friend, Sylvia.

59
00:03:53,032 --> 00:03:54,744
Someone who listens and helps

60
00:03:54,768 --> 00:03:56,512
put things in
perspective. That's all.

61
00:03:56,536 --> 00:03:58,548
Still it's quite an
accomplishment

62
00:03:58,572 --> 00:04:01,751
to be syndicated in every
major newspaper in the U.S.

63
00:04:01,775 --> 00:04:03,587
What's the secret
of your success?

64
00:04:03,611 --> 00:04:06,722
A little intuition
and a lot of luck.

65
00:04:06,746 --> 00:04:10,293
And the uncompromising
love and support I receive daily

66
00:04:10,317 --> 00:04:13,196
from my wonderful family,
who are with me here today.

67
00:04:13,220 --> 00:04:15,931
Marjorie... And not
to be overlooked

68
00:04:15,955 --> 00:04:17,066
are two very special people:

69
00:04:17,090 --> 00:04:19,935
our terrific kids...
Randall and Lucia,

70
00:04:19,959 --> 00:04:22,739
who grew up altogether too
fast, and are away at college.

71
00:04:22,763 --> 00:04:24,941
And then of course there are

72
00:04:24,965 --> 00:04:26,142
the people that I work with

73
00:04:26,166 --> 00:04:28,078
at the<i> Examiner,</i> who
have become like family.

74
00:04:28,102 --> 00:04:31,347
Nora Gould, our editor,
who could not be here today.

75
00:04:31,371 --> 00:04:35,284
And Harold... our
ever-ready copy boy.

76
00:04:35,308 --> 00:04:37,220
Always Johnny-on-the-spot.

77
00:04:38,312 --> 00:04:40,278
Any words of advice
for our viewers?

78
00:04:42,850 --> 00:04:48,765
My advice... to everyone
watching today is,

79
00:04:48,789 --> 00:04:50,700
cherish your loved ones...

80
00:04:50,724 --> 00:04:51,901
no matter who you are,

81
00:04:51,925 --> 00:04:54,504
or where you are,
or what you do.

82
00:04:54,528 --> 00:04:57,140
They are life's most
precious commodity.

83
00:04:57,164 --> 00:05:00,477
As usual, Marjorie, you've
given us something to think about.

84
00:05:00,501 --> 00:05:01,644
Again, congratulations.

85
00:05:01,668 --> 00:05:03,680
Thank you.

86
00:05:03,704 --> 00:05:05,448
This is Sylvia
Richland, coming to you

87
00:05:05,472 --> 00:05:07,249
from the home of
Marjorie Manners,

88
00:05:07,273 --> 00:05:10,253
this year's recipient of
Atlanta's Golden Peach Award.

89
00:05:11,878 --> 00:05:13,556
Cut.

90
00:05:13,580 --> 00:05:15,624
Bravo, bravo, bravo. Thank you.

91
00:05:15,648 --> 00:05:17,894
Oh, thank you, sweetheart.
You were wonderful, my love.

92
00:05:17,918 --> 00:05:18,995
Congratulations. Carol.

93
00:05:19,019 --> 00:05:20,229
Congratulations.

94
00:05:20,253 --> 00:05:21,698
I'm proud of you.
Congratulations.

95
00:05:21,722 --> 00:05:23,465
Couldn't have happened
to a nicer landlady.

96
00:05:23,489 --> 00:05:26,102
Thank you, Gideon. Help
yourself to some champagne.

97
00:05:26,126 --> 00:05:28,237
Thanks a lot. Harold,

98
00:05:28,261 --> 00:05:29,839
some very dear and
old friends of mine

99
00:05:29,863 --> 00:05:31,007
have asked me out to dinner.

100
00:05:31,031 --> 00:05:32,809
And what with receiving
the award and all,

101
00:05:32,833 --> 00:05:35,544
I'm afraid I haven't had
time to do tomorrow's column.

102
00:05:35,568 --> 00:05:39,415
The copy will be ready at
10 instead of 6 tonight, okay?

103
00:05:39,439 --> 00:05:40,984
Mm, no problem,
Ms. Manners. Sure.

104
00:05:41,008 --> 00:05:42,786
All right, where is she?

105
00:05:42,810 --> 00:05:45,510
Where's the famous answer lady?

106
00:05:46,847 --> 00:05:50,093
You are destroying my
life. Do you know that?

107
00:05:50,117 --> 00:05:51,194
Who are you?

108
00:05:51,218 --> 00:05:52,495
I'm Craig Moore.

109
00:05:52,519 --> 00:05:55,364
Because of her, my
wife is gonna leave me.

110
00:05:55,388 --> 00:05:56,833
How can you do that?

111
00:05:56,857 --> 00:05:59,101
You... You treat her
fantasies like they're real.

112
00:05:59,125 --> 00:06:00,837
She actually believes you.

113
00:06:00,861 --> 00:06:02,439
Carter, help me get rid of him.

114
00:06:02,463 --> 00:06:03,739
Stay back.

115
00:06:03,763 --> 00:06:06,076
I came here to have it
out with her. Nobody else.

116
00:06:06,100 --> 00:06:07,076
I'm calling the police.

117
00:06:07,100 --> 00:06:09,100
No, Carol, please. Wait.

118
00:06:10,938 --> 00:06:13,216
What's your name again?

119
00:06:13,240 --> 00:06:15,185
Craig... Moore.

120
00:06:15,209 --> 00:06:17,886
Craig, I've obviously
done something

121
00:06:17,910 --> 00:06:20,557
to make you very angry,
and for that, I'm sorry.

122
00:06:20,581 --> 00:06:22,525
Let's talk, shall we?

123
00:06:22,549 --> 00:06:24,493
In there. Just the two of us.

124
00:06:24,517 --> 00:06:26,229
You can't do that.
The man's deranged.

125
00:06:26,253 --> 00:06:27,630
I don't think so.

126
00:06:27,654 --> 00:06:30,422
If anything, I'm a pretty
good judge of character.

127
00:06:32,292 --> 00:06:35,227
Come on, son. Let's talk.

128
00:06:55,349 --> 00:06:58,661
Those people out there
are very special to me, Craig.

129
00:06:58,685 --> 00:07:00,496
And we were just about
to celebrate something

130
00:07:00,520 --> 00:07:02,298
that will happen only
once in my lifetime,

131
00:07:02,322 --> 00:07:03,833
and no doubt never in yours.

132
00:07:03,857 --> 00:07:06,002
So if it's all the same to you,

133
00:07:06,026 --> 00:07:07,336
I'd like to get this over with.

134
00:07:07,360 --> 00:07:08,705
What the hell do you want?

135
00:07:08,729 --> 00:07:11,574
I just want you to
straighten out Anna.

136
00:07:11,598 --> 00:07:13,609
Well, you know her as
In Love, But Miserable.

137
00:07:13,633 --> 00:07:14,677
What do you want me to do?

138
00:07:14,701 --> 00:07:16,446
Print a retraction.

139
00:07:16,470 --> 00:07:17,903
Retraction?

140
00:07:19,006 --> 00:07:21,384
Who do I look like,
Bob Woodward?

141
00:07:21,408 --> 00:07:23,920
I give advice to the
lovelorn. For crying out loud.

142
00:07:23,944 --> 00:07:25,922
Well, then print a modification.

143
00:07:25,946 --> 00:07:27,226
I think you'd better leave.

144
00:07:28,048 --> 00:07:29,625
You could write
an open letter...

145
00:07:29,649 --> 00:07:31,961
saying you've been giving In
Love, But Miserable's problem

146
00:07:31,985 --> 00:07:34,431
more thought, and... And
you think she'd be better off

147
00:07:34,455 --> 00:07:35,832
staying with her
husband after all.

148
00:07:35,856 --> 00:07:37,099
A-and then all
the crazy ideas...

149
00:07:37,123 --> 00:07:39,001
I don't have time for this now.

150
00:07:39,025 --> 00:07:40,091
Well, I'll come back.

151
00:07:41,895 --> 00:07:43,773
Fine. You come back here tonight

152
00:07:43,797 --> 00:07:45,841
and we'll write this
open letter together.

153
00:07:45,865 --> 00:07:46,943
Does that do it?

154
00:07:46,967 --> 00:07:48,445
You bet.

155
00:07:48,469 --> 00:07:49,946
Good.

156
00:07:49,970 --> 00:07:51,702
Now, smile.

157
00:08:01,014 --> 00:08:02,892
Ten o'clock
tonight in my office.

158
00:08:02,916 --> 00:08:04,260
It's the bungalow by the pool.

159
00:08:04,284 --> 00:08:05,828
Just come straight back.

160
00:08:05,852 --> 00:08:07,919
I will. Thank you.

161
00:08:09,255 --> 00:08:11,501
D...? Did I hear you correctly?

162
00:08:11,525 --> 00:08:13,403
Did you just invite
him back here?

163
00:08:13,427 --> 00:08:14,938
Marjorie, the man
threatened you.

164
00:08:14,962 --> 00:08:16,506
He's harmless.

165
00:08:16,530 --> 00:08:18,307
Like so many people,
all he really wants

166
00:08:18,331 --> 00:08:21,198
is someone to
listen and to care.

167
00:08:24,237 --> 00:08:25,314
Drink up. You deserve it.

168
00:09:45,953 --> 00:09:47,152
Ms. Manners?

169
00:10:06,740 --> 00:10:07,740
I...

170
00:10:30,196 --> 00:10:34,777
Um... Good morning, Cassie.

171
00:10:34,801 --> 00:10:37,135
Good morning.

172
00:10:42,475 --> 00:10:44,586
Problems with her boyfriend. Oh.

173
00:10:44,610 --> 00:10:46,389
Or at least, she thought
he was her boyfriend.

174
00:10:46,413 --> 00:10:47,990
They've been going
out for about a month.

175
00:10:48,014 --> 00:10:49,791
Suddenly, he just
stopped calling.

176
00:10:49,815 --> 00:10:52,361
Oh. You think he, ahem,
what, dropped her? Huh?

177
00:10:52,385 --> 00:10:53,985
Something like that. Mm.

178
00:10:57,824 --> 00:11:00,264
I'm sorry, Ben. That
won't happen again.

179
00:11:01,227 --> 00:11:04,139
As far as I'm concerned,
Brad is history.

180
00:11:04,163 --> 00:11:05,863
Good for you.

181
00:11:10,003 --> 00:11:13,949
So what if he looks like
Tom Cruise with curly hair?

182
00:11:13,973 --> 00:11:17,185
So what if he has a
great sense of humor?

183
00:11:17,209 --> 00:11:19,889
So what if we have absolutely
everything in common?

184
00:11:19,913 --> 00:11:21,791
He can take a flying leap.

185
00:11:21,815 --> 00:11:24,226
Well... why don't you call him?

186
00:11:24,250 --> 00:11:27,263
Because he said he'd call me.

187
00:11:27,287 --> 00:11:30,366
And besides, I
don't care anymore.

188
00:11:30,390 --> 00:11:32,334
Oh.

189
00:11:32,358 --> 00:11:33,558
Well, we'd better go to court.

190
00:11:54,514 --> 00:11:55,758
What do you say
we call the office

191
00:11:55,782 --> 00:11:56,926
and go straight to lunch?

192
00:11:56,950 --> 00:11:58,160
Oh, yeah. Yeah. I'm starved.

193
00:11:58,184 --> 00:11:59,729
Mr. Matlock? Hm?

194
00:11:59,753 --> 00:12:01,363
My name is Anna Moore.

195
00:12:01,387 --> 00:12:04,033
My husband is Craig Moore.
The man who's been charged

196
00:12:04,057 --> 00:12:06,402
with the murder of
Marjorie Manners.

197
00:12:06,426 --> 00:12:08,692
I called your office, but
there was no answer.

198
00:12:09,896 --> 00:12:11,173
There wasn't?

199
00:12:11,197 --> 00:12:13,408
My husband needs
a good attorney.

200
00:12:13,432 --> 00:12:14,943
Oh, um, Ms. Moore,

201
00:12:14,967 --> 00:12:18,180
the state's case against
your husband is rock solid.

202
00:12:18,204 --> 00:12:21,183
I know. They've got
fingerprints, witnesses, motive.

203
00:12:21,207 --> 00:12:23,552
And God knows he
isn't much of a saint.

204
00:12:23,576 --> 00:12:25,487
But my husband isn't a murderer.

205
00:12:25,511 --> 00:12:28,256
Mr. Matlock, you have
to understand something.

206
00:12:28,280 --> 00:12:29,625
I'm rich.

207
00:12:29,649 --> 00:12:33,262
My father left me a trust
fund of over $20 million.

208
00:12:33,286 --> 00:12:36,131
I can pay you any fee
you want, just name it.

209
00:12:36,155 --> 00:12:38,534
W-w-well, wait... Wait a minute.

210
00:12:38,558 --> 00:12:40,402
If your husband thought

211
00:12:40,426 --> 00:12:41,804
he was gonna lose all that money

212
00:12:41,828 --> 00:12:45,775
because of some advice
Marjorie Manners gave you

213
00:12:45,799 --> 00:12:48,944
in her column, that's
a very powerful motive.

214
00:12:48,968 --> 00:12:51,948
I offered all of my
money to Craig years ago.

215
00:12:51,972 --> 00:12:53,416
He wouldn't take it.

216
00:12:53,440 --> 00:12:56,952
He even signed a
prenuptial agreement.

217
00:12:56,976 --> 00:12:58,654
And then I said let's
live on my fortune.

218
00:12:58,678 --> 00:13:01,657
He said, "No I'd
like a nice office job."

219
00:13:05,085 --> 00:13:08,152
He wasn't in danger of
losing you or the marriage?

220
00:13:09,656 --> 00:13:11,300
Well... yes.

221
00:13:11,324 --> 00:13:14,436
I... threatened leaving him,

222
00:13:14,460 --> 00:13:18,295
just to get him to face up to
what was going on between us.

223
00:13:19,365 --> 00:13:20,409
What was that?

224
00:13:20,433 --> 00:13:22,211
It's very personal.

225
00:13:22,235 --> 00:13:24,380
You said, uh, a little while ago

226
00:13:24,404 --> 00:13:27,650
that he was no saint.

227
00:13:27,674 --> 00:13:28,973
What did that mean?

228
00:13:30,777 --> 00:13:33,510
Not in front of her.

229
00:13:35,182 --> 00:13:36,847
Excuse me.

230
00:13:39,752 --> 00:13:42,297
This is privileged
information, right?

231
00:13:42,321 --> 00:13:43,499
Yeah.

232
00:13:43,523 --> 00:13:45,801
There's something about Craig.

233
00:13:45,825 --> 00:13:49,305
I'm not even sure what it
is, but it's very powerful.

234
00:13:49,329 --> 00:13:51,274
Women can't resist him.

235
00:13:51,298 --> 00:13:55,211
They are constantly
throwing themselves at him.

236
00:13:55,235 --> 00:13:57,580
And Craig is only human.

237
00:13:57,604 --> 00:13:59,748
He succumbs.

238
00:13:59,772 --> 00:14:02,585
He's... unfaithful?

239
00:14:02,609 --> 00:14:04,353
Dozens of times.

240
00:14:04,377 --> 00:14:06,755
Do you have proof?

241
00:14:06,779 --> 00:14:09,758
A woman doesn't need proof
of that sort of thing, Mr. Matlock.

242
00:14:09,782 --> 00:14:11,460
She knows.

243
00:14:11,484 --> 00:14:13,696
But despite this incredible,

244
00:14:13,720 --> 00:14:17,099
this unexplainable magnetism,

245
00:14:17,123 --> 00:14:19,267
Craig is a wonderful,

246
00:14:19,291 --> 00:14:22,705
if occasionally weak, man.

247
00:14:22,729 --> 00:14:24,740
I just love him.

248
00:14:24,764 --> 00:14:26,642
Will you take the case?

249
00:14:30,604 --> 00:14:32,214
Bail is set at $50,000.

250
00:14:32,238 --> 00:14:33,816
The preliminary
hearing will take place

251
00:14:33,840 --> 00:14:36,919
in this courtroom on March 12th.

252
00:14:38,979 --> 00:14:40,556
Your wife's made
arrangements for bail.

253
00:14:40,580 --> 00:14:42,391
You should be out
of here within an hour.

254
00:14:42,415 --> 00:14:46,795
Mr. Matlock, I'm
going to be frank.

255
00:14:46,819 --> 00:14:48,364
I don't think your associate

256
00:14:48,388 --> 00:14:50,454
should be involved
with this case.

257
00:14:52,359 --> 00:14:54,570
Isn't it obvious?

258
00:14:54,594 --> 00:14:56,271
Well, not that I
blame her, though,

259
00:14:56,295 --> 00:14:58,708
but she won't be
able to resist him.

260
00:14:58,732 --> 00:15:00,843
Oh, well, she'll resist him.

261
00:15:00,867 --> 00:15:02,778
She's a professional.

262
00:15:02,802 --> 00:15:04,280
Anna...

263
00:15:04,304 --> 00:15:07,717
Michelle said I'll be out
of here within an hour.

264
00:15:07,741 --> 00:15:11,575
The big question is,
then can I come home?

265
00:15:16,182 --> 00:15:18,949
Not until you at least
admit we have a problem.

266
00:15:20,019 --> 00:15:23,165
Anna... I love you.

267
00:15:23,189 --> 00:15:25,167
I have never, nor will I ever,

268
00:15:25,191 --> 00:15:27,271
have an affair
with someone else.

269
00:15:32,932 --> 00:15:35,372
I'll be at the hotel.

270
00:15:40,307 --> 00:15:42,940
Isn't he wonderful?

271
00:15:54,353 --> 00:15:55,865
Hm.

272
00:15:55,889 --> 00:15:57,065
Oh.

273
00:15:57,089 --> 00:15:58,834
Pool house was her office, huh?

274
00:15:58,858 --> 00:16:00,102
Yeah.

275
00:16:00,126 --> 00:16:01,737
Lieutenant Daniels
left it unlocked.

276
00:16:01,761 --> 00:16:02,738
Yeah.

277
00:16:02,762 --> 00:16:04,128
Here.

278
00:16:19,211 --> 00:16:22,425
Think we surprised
ourselves a burglar?

279
00:16:22,449 --> 00:16:23,793
What kind of burglar would leave

280
00:16:23,817 --> 00:16:25,060
something like this behind?

281
00:16:25,084 --> 00:16:26,150
Ah.

282
00:16:27,720 --> 00:16:31,233
Maybe he was looking
for something in here.

283
00:16:31,257 --> 00:16:32,667
You think maybe
we walked in on him

284
00:16:32,691 --> 00:16:34,436
before he found what
he was looking for?

285
00:16:34,460 --> 00:16:36,272
It'd be good.

286
00:16:36,296 --> 00:16:38,540
You know, you don't need me
to meet with the family members.

287
00:16:38,564 --> 00:16:40,609
Why don't I just stay
here and look around?

288
00:16:40,633 --> 00:16:42,244
Sure.

289
00:16:42,268 --> 00:16:43,700
Look here.

290
00:16:45,238 --> 00:16:47,182
There's a picture of
the president up here.

291
00:16:47,206 --> 00:16:49,185
Yeah. It's autographed
and everything.

292
00:16:49,209 --> 00:16:51,575
Yeah, her column
was pretty popular.

293
00:16:53,179 --> 00:16:55,191
I've never read it.

294
00:16:55,215 --> 00:16:59,195
Well maybe that's why he's
up there, and you're down here.

295
00:17:07,460 --> 00:17:08,437
Mr. Matlock.

296
00:17:08,461 --> 00:17:09,538
Yes, sir.

297
00:17:09,562 --> 00:17:10,573
Lee Manners.

298
00:17:10,597 --> 00:17:12,208
Very nice to meet you.

299
00:17:12,232 --> 00:17:14,543
They're good-looking
kids. They yours?

300
00:17:14,567 --> 00:17:17,679
Oh, thank you. Yes,
uh... Randall's at Duke

301
00:17:17,703 --> 00:17:19,582
and Lucia's at Baxter.

302
00:17:19,606 --> 00:17:20,782
Hm.

303
00:17:20,806 --> 00:17:24,987
Marjorie was very
proud of both of 'em.

304
00:17:25,011 --> 00:17:28,124
This is a very awkward
time for you to be paying us

305
00:17:28,148 --> 00:17:30,126
this visit, Mr. Matlock.

306
00:17:30,150 --> 00:17:31,550
We're all still in
a state of shock.

307
00:17:32,885 --> 00:17:34,630
Some things don't
change, darling.

308
00:17:37,356 --> 00:17:41,672
We...

309
00:17:42,495 --> 00:17:44,473
You must be Ben Matlock?

310
00:17:44,497 --> 00:17:45,507
Yes.

311
00:17:45,531 --> 00:17:46,642
I'm Carol Weston. Hello.

312
00:17:46,666 --> 00:17:47,743
That's my husband, Carter.

313
00:17:47,767 --> 00:17:49,377
Hello. Hello.

314
00:17:49,401 --> 00:17:50,546
I'm sorry we're late.

315
00:17:50,570 --> 00:17:51,847
We didn't know you were here.

316
00:17:51,871 --> 00:17:53,682
What's this all
about, Mr. Matlock?

317
00:17:53,706 --> 00:17:55,517
Oh, just to say hello.

318
00:17:55,541 --> 00:17:58,219
I know this is a bad
time, and I apologize,

319
00:17:58,243 --> 00:18:00,589
but I... I have to
follow up on something.

320
00:18:00,613 --> 00:18:03,392
It... It occurred to me that
my client, Craig Moore,

321
00:18:03,416 --> 00:18:06,194
was framed, and if that's so,

322
00:18:06,218 --> 00:18:09,498
then whoever actually killed
Marjorie had to have heard her

323
00:18:09,522 --> 00:18:12,835
tell him to come back
here at 10:00 that night.

324
00:18:12,859 --> 00:18:15,437
I don't think your client
was framed, Mr. Matlock.

325
00:18:15,461 --> 00:18:16,571
I think he killed Marjorie,

326
00:18:16,595 --> 00:18:18,006
and I think you
really should leave.

327
00:18:18,030 --> 00:18:19,241
Absolutely.

328
00:18:19,265 --> 00:18:21,343
Well, I thought rather
than depose all of you

329
00:18:21,367 --> 00:18:24,647
or haul you into court, it might
be more convenient for you

330
00:18:24,671 --> 00:18:26,415
to answer my questions here.

331
00:18:26,439 --> 00:18:28,984
Excuse, me. Uh, y... I
mean, you don't seriously think

332
00:18:29,008 --> 00:18:30,386
that it was one of us?

333
00:18:30,410 --> 00:18:31,821
Lee,

334
00:18:31,845 --> 00:18:33,856
this is just some sort
of legal smoke screen.

335
00:18:33,880 --> 00:18:35,724
No, it... It isn't that.

336
00:18:35,748 --> 00:18:36,958
Uh, somebody did kill Marjorie.

337
00:18:36,982 --> 00:18:40,862
And I don't think it
was Craig Moore.

338
00:18:40,886 --> 00:18:43,933
But now that I've
seen all of you...

339
00:18:43,957 --> 00:18:46,102
Husband, sister, brother-in-law

340
00:18:46,126 --> 00:18:49,305
all living together
under the same roof,

341
00:18:49,329 --> 00:18:51,506
I can see that you
have to be ruled out.

342
00:18:51,530 --> 00:18:55,578
Uh, you're just one
big, happy family.

343
00:18:55,602 --> 00:18:58,180
Again, Mr. Matlock, I
hardly think that happy

344
00:18:58,204 --> 00:18:59,714
is an apt description
of any of us,

345
00:18:59,738 --> 00:19:01,716
under the circumstances.

346
00:19:01,740 --> 00:19:03,752
I'm sorry. I have
an appointment.

347
00:19:03,776 --> 00:19:04,986
Yes, and I have to get to class.

348
00:19:05,010 --> 00:19:06,621
I'd like to skip it,
but I'm the professor.

349
00:19:06,645 --> 00:19:08,023
Well, I'll move along too.

350
00:19:08,047 --> 00:19:11,460
Um, I just, as I say,
wanted to say hello.

351
00:19:11,484 --> 00:19:12,928
Could I use your telephone?

352
00:19:12,952 --> 00:19:14,729
Yeah. Somebody was, uh, rifling

353
00:19:14,753 --> 00:19:16,065
through Marjorie's office,

354
00:19:16,089 --> 00:19:17,565
and I need to
report it to the police.

355
00:19:17,589 --> 00:19:21,070
I don't suppose one of you
knows who it might have been?

356
00:19:21,094 --> 00:19:24,273
No, I... Or what they
were looking for?

357
00:19:24,297 --> 00:19:26,542
Mr. Matlock, I can't imagine.

358
00:19:26,566 --> 00:19:27,910
Neither can I.

359
00:19:27,934 --> 00:19:30,012
Well. Yeah.

360
00:19:36,075 --> 00:19:38,287
You're too pretty
to be a thief...

361
00:19:38,311 --> 00:19:41,257
or a cop.

362
00:19:41,281 --> 00:19:45,093
And you're obviously
not the cleaning lady.

363
00:19:45,117 --> 00:19:46,895
<i>Atlanta Examiner?</i>

364
00:19:46,919 --> 00:19:48,263
No.

365
00:19:48,287 --> 00:19:51,032
I'm one of the attorneys
representing Craig Moore.

366
00:19:51,056 --> 00:19:52,101
Michelle Thomas.

367
00:19:52,125 --> 00:19:53,302
And you're...?

368
00:19:53,326 --> 00:19:54,303
Gideon Harris.

369
00:19:54,327 --> 00:19:55,704
I've got a room upstairs.

370
00:19:55,728 --> 00:19:58,006
Do you rent it out, or are
you a member of the family?

371
00:19:58,030 --> 00:19:59,030
Rent.

372
00:20:00,867 --> 00:20:02,411
Although I must say,

373
00:20:02,435 --> 00:20:04,914
Marjorie had this way of making
even poor college students

374
00:20:04,938 --> 00:20:08,116
working on their Ph.D.s
feel like they were at home.

375
00:20:08,140 --> 00:20:09,985
Good lady.

376
00:20:10,009 --> 00:20:12,354
Um, about 20 minutes ago

377
00:20:12,378 --> 00:20:15,323
someone was in here
tearing this place apart.

378
00:20:15,347 --> 00:20:16,825
I don't suppose you saw anybody?

379
00:20:16,849 --> 00:20:18,816
No. I just got back
from the library.

380
00:20:19,985 --> 00:20:21,196
Although I do have a clear view

381
00:20:21,220 --> 00:20:22,664
of this room from my room.

382
00:20:22,688 --> 00:20:24,455
That's how I knew you were here.

383
00:20:29,895 --> 00:20:30,973
See?

384
00:20:30,997 --> 00:20:32,975
Oh.

385
00:20:32,999 --> 00:20:37,613
Whoever was in here
just might come back.

386
00:20:37,637 --> 00:20:40,282
How amenable would you
be to my staking out this place

387
00:20:40,306 --> 00:20:41,450
from your room?

388
00:20:41,474 --> 00:20:42,451
Just for a while.

389
00:20:42,475 --> 00:20:44,085
Till midnight at the most.

390
00:20:44,109 --> 00:20:45,287
Sounds good to me.

391
00:20:45,311 --> 00:20:47,355
Great. I really appreciate it.

392
00:20:47,379 --> 00:20:48,857
I'll just throw a
bottle of chardonnay

393
00:20:48,881 --> 00:20:51,321
in the fridge, and
we'll be all set.

394
00:21:03,596 --> 00:21:06,809
Well, now you can say you've
dined at Gideon's playroom.

395
00:21:06,833 --> 00:21:08,243
Great.

396
00:21:08,267 --> 00:21:09,645
No, no. I meant that the Manners

397
00:21:09,669 --> 00:21:11,880
used this as a
playroom for their kids.

398
00:21:11,904 --> 00:21:13,281
Oh.

399
00:21:13,305 --> 00:21:15,217
How long have
you been renting it?

400
00:21:15,241 --> 00:21:16,819
Just two years. That's
when Carter heard

401
00:21:16,843 --> 00:21:19,321
I needed a place to stay,
and he offered it to me.

402
00:21:19,345 --> 00:21:20,989
I met him down
at the university.

403
00:21:21,013 --> 00:21:22,424
Mm. Sure you wouldn't like

404
00:21:22,448 --> 00:21:24,759
some wine? You only
got a half hour to go.

405
00:21:24,783 --> 00:21:26,917
Uh, thanks, but no thanks.

406
00:21:31,690 --> 00:21:34,130
Of course, you're
welcome to stay longer.

407
00:21:36,829 --> 00:21:37,906
Sorry. I'll shut up.

408
00:21:44,671 --> 00:21:46,282
Is that your dissertation?

409
00:21:46,306 --> 00:21:48,651
Yeah. I just finished
the rough draft.

410
00:21:48,675 --> 00:21:49,685
Tsk.

411
00:21:49,709 --> 00:21:51,720
Not a minute too soon.

412
00:21:51,744 --> 00:21:53,188
What do you mean?

413
00:21:53,212 --> 00:21:54,623
I'm a sociologist.

414
00:21:54,647 --> 00:21:57,025
I'm specializing in the
effects of mass communication

415
00:21:57,049 --> 00:21:58,460
on modern culture.

416
00:21:58,484 --> 00:22:02,064
Specifically, I'm studying the
influence of advice columns.

417
00:22:02,088 --> 00:22:04,967
Don't tell me your dissertation
is on Marjorie Manners?

418
00:22:06,158 --> 00:22:07,536
Hey, don't knock
it till you read it.

419
00:22:07,560 --> 00:22:08,837
It's good.

420
00:22:08,861 --> 00:22:11,140
It's gonna get me a
teaching position at Yale.

421
00:22:14,433 --> 00:22:16,553
Sounds like Lee just got home.

422
00:22:23,175 --> 00:22:25,521
We're supposed to be
watching the pool house.

423
00:22:25,545 --> 00:22:27,623
Watching the main house

424
00:22:27,647 --> 00:22:29,947
is much more
interesting, believe me.

425
00:22:31,484 --> 00:22:34,596
Yeah, off goes his light...

426
00:22:34,620 --> 00:22:36,253
and on goes hers.

427
00:22:37,523 --> 00:22:38,667
Whose?

428
00:22:38,691 --> 00:22:39,890
Carol's.

429
00:22:41,327 --> 00:22:44,440
Now, what room's it
gonna be in tonight?

430
00:22:44,464 --> 00:22:46,797
Ah. Maybe the study.

431
00:22:51,704 --> 00:22:53,281
What are you talking about?

432
00:22:54,440 --> 00:22:55,751
How important is
it for you to know

433
00:22:55,775 --> 00:22:58,987
what Atlanta's perfect
family is really like?

434
00:22:59,011 --> 00:23:00,155
Very.

435
00:23:00,179 --> 00:23:01,299
Come on.

436
00:23:50,996 --> 00:23:52,040
Gideon.

437
00:23:52,064 --> 00:23:53,575
Uh, I'm sorry, Lee.

438
00:23:53,599 --> 00:23:54,776
I saw the light on in here.

439
00:23:54,800 --> 00:23:57,846
I thought I'd just
come in and get this.

440
00:23:57,870 --> 00:23:59,014
Have you met Michelle Thomas?

441
00:23:59,038 --> 00:24:01,049
She works for Ben Matlock.

442
00:24:01,073 --> 00:24:03,752
Michelle, this is Carol
Weston, Marjorie's sister,

443
00:24:03,776 --> 00:24:05,587
and Lee Manners,
Marjorie's husband.

444
00:24:05,611 --> 00:24:06,955
How do you do?

445
00:24:06,979 --> 00:24:08,356
Hello.

446
00:24:08,380 --> 00:24:10,940
Carter working late tonight?

447
00:24:11,216 --> 00:24:13,896
A, uh... An
interdepartmental meeting.

448
00:24:14,921 --> 00:24:17,065
I was waiting up for him.

449
00:24:34,206 --> 00:24:38,287
Gideon. For God's
sakes, let go of me.

450
00:24:38,311 --> 00:24:40,155
Michelle Thomas, Carter Weston,

451
00:24:40,179 --> 00:24:41,323
Marjorie's brother-in-law.

452
00:24:42,381 --> 00:24:43,759
Uh, I'm an attorney.

453
00:24:43,783 --> 00:24:46,028
I work with Ben Matlock.

454
00:24:46,052 --> 00:24:47,562
I thought you had one of those

455
00:24:47,586 --> 00:24:50,765
late-night
interdepartmental meetings.

456
00:24:50,789 --> 00:24:52,134
I left early.

457
00:24:52,158 --> 00:24:54,303
Uh, what were you doing here?

458
00:24:54,327 --> 00:24:57,672
I, uh, was looking for something
that I lent to Marjorie: a book.

459
00:24:57,696 --> 00:24:58,928
With a flashlight?

460
00:25:03,302 --> 00:25:05,822
Crossing police lines
without authorization?

461
00:25:07,172 --> 00:25:09,383
Now, can you give me any
reason why I shouldn't just pick up

462
00:25:09,407 --> 00:25:12,275
the telephone and
report this to the police?

463
00:25:17,549 --> 00:25:19,628
Gideon, if you'll
excuse us, please.

464
00:25:19,652 --> 00:25:21,384
Sure.

465
00:25:24,423 --> 00:25:26,156
I'll be at my place
if you need me.

466
00:25:33,098 --> 00:25:36,011
In case you're curious,
it was me in here earlier.

467
00:25:36,035 --> 00:25:39,214
I was looking for
Marjorie's diary.

468
00:25:39,238 --> 00:25:40,537
Why?

469
00:25:42,141 --> 00:25:45,053
I'm gonna give you
the short version.

470
00:25:45,077 --> 00:25:47,477
My marriage is all I have left.

471
00:25:49,081 --> 00:25:51,759
I don't want to lose Carol.

472
00:25:51,783 --> 00:25:55,752
Losing Marjorie was...
traumatic enough.

473
00:25:57,622 --> 00:25:59,901
Marjorie and I were
having an affair.

474
00:26:04,830 --> 00:26:07,864
It was her idea
from the start, but...

475
00:26:09,368 --> 00:26:12,736
But I did grow accustomed
to the arrangement.

476
00:26:14,840 --> 00:26:17,786
Now that... Now that
Marjorie is out of my life,

477
00:26:17,810 --> 00:26:19,943
I don't want Carol to find out.

478
00:26:21,780 --> 00:26:24,492
Now, I'm gonna have to
leave this up to your discretion,

479
00:26:24,516 --> 00:26:27,128
but... please.

480
00:26:27,152 --> 00:26:28,752
Carol is all I have left.

481
00:26:38,964 --> 00:26:40,576
Well, I'm on my way
over to the university

482
00:26:40,600 --> 00:26:43,011
to check out Gideon Harris.

483
00:26:43,035 --> 00:26:44,613
You know, the guy
that lives upstairs.

484
00:26:44,637 --> 00:26:45,747
Oh. He says he was

485
00:26:45,771 --> 00:26:47,549
at a seminar, so we'll see.

486
00:26:47,573 --> 00:26:48,750
Yeah.

487
00:27:07,593 --> 00:27:09,071
You gonna answer that?

488
00:27:09,095 --> 00:27:11,061
What if it's Brad?

489
00:27:12,131 --> 00:27:13,296
Hello?

490
00:27:14,834 --> 00:27:16,867
No, she just left.

491
00:27:18,203 --> 00:27:19,948
Uh, yes. I'll tell her.

492
00:27:19,972 --> 00:27:21,104
Bye.

493
00:27:22,475 --> 00:27:24,486
Ahem. I thought you
and Brad were history.

494
00:27:24,510 --> 00:27:28,123
Well, I finally called
him, and it turns out

495
00:27:28,147 --> 00:27:30,058
he goes out of town
a lot on business,

496
00:27:30,082 --> 00:27:32,660
and that's why I
hadn't heard from him.

497
00:27:32,684 --> 00:27:35,351
And that's also why I'm
afraid to answer the phone.

498
00:27:36,655 --> 00:27:38,000
I don't get it.

499
00:27:38,024 --> 00:27:39,868
Well, he's got another
business trip coming up,

500
00:27:39,892 --> 00:27:41,803
and he wants me to go with him.

501
00:27:41,827 --> 00:27:43,526
Oh.

502
00:27:45,664 --> 00:27:48,376
I told him that I... I'd
have to think about it.

503
00:27:48,400 --> 00:27:49,680
What do you think?

504
00:27:50,569 --> 00:27:52,102
I don't know.

505
00:27:53,772 --> 00:27:55,750
Well, if I go...

506
00:27:55,774 --> 00:27:58,374
I know it will change
our relationship radically.

507
00:28:01,413 --> 00:28:02,612
What do you think?

508
00:28:03,682 --> 00:28:04,960
Well, I don't know.

509
00:28:04,984 --> 00:28:07,216
Do you think I ought to go?

510
00:28:09,288 --> 00:28:10,866
Ahem. Maybe you ought to go

511
00:28:10,890 --> 00:28:14,436
and stay in two separate
rooms and meet him at breakfast.

512
00:28:16,462 --> 00:28:19,641
I'll be out at the
Manners' estate.

513
00:28:25,171 --> 00:28:27,983
You know, this is
kind of an odd family.

514
00:28:28,007 --> 00:28:29,851
Mr. Matlock,

515
00:28:29,875 --> 00:28:32,921
Marjorie could be
very, uh, vindictive.

516
00:28:32,945 --> 00:28:35,957
I adored her when
we were first married,

517
00:28:35,981 --> 00:28:37,492
but the more popular
her column became,

518
00:28:37,516 --> 00:28:39,961
the more miserable she
made everyone around her.

519
00:28:39,985 --> 00:28:43,065
She... She became
enthralled with her own power.

520
00:28:43,089 --> 00:28:45,567
She made more
money than anyone else,

521
00:28:45,591 --> 00:28:47,202
so she insisted
that I give up my job

522
00:28:47,226 --> 00:28:48,704
and stay home with the kids.

523
00:28:48,728 --> 00:28:51,039
She even made
Carol stop working.

524
00:28:51,063 --> 00:28:53,375
She wanted to
control everything.

525
00:28:53,399 --> 00:28:55,376
Hm.

526
00:28:55,400 --> 00:28:57,879
She let Carter keep working.

527
00:28:57,903 --> 00:29:00,716
Only because his
salary was so laughable.

528
00:29:00,740 --> 00:29:04,519
But what's inexcusable isn't
so much what she did to us,

529
00:29:04,543 --> 00:29:08,623
but... what she did to our kids.

530
00:29:08,647 --> 00:29:10,558
They're as screwed
up as they come.

531
00:29:10,582 --> 00:29:11,927
Oh, that's right.

532
00:29:11,951 --> 00:29:14,863
They didn't even come
home when their mother died.

533
00:29:14,887 --> 00:29:16,531
One I can't even find,

534
00:29:16,555 --> 00:29:19,901
and, uh, Randall,
the other one...

535
00:29:19,925 --> 00:29:22,125
he'd rather not see any of us.

536
00:29:25,364 --> 00:29:28,843
That's a shame.
That's really a shame.

537
00:29:28,867 --> 00:29:30,934
I'm sorry.

538
00:29:32,337 --> 00:29:37,251
You know, sometimes
I feel awful...

539
00:29:37,275 --> 00:29:41,144
but I have to ask where you
were the night of the murder.

540
00:29:42,214 --> 00:29:44,125
I was home, alone.

541
00:29:44,149 --> 00:29:46,461
Can anybody corroborate that?

542
00:29:46,485 --> 00:29:49,045
No. Nobody was here.

543
00:29:49,287 --> 00:29:50,565
Where was Carter?

544
00:29:50,589 --> 00:29:54,069
Carter? Well, he said he
was going to the library.

545
00:29:54,093 --> 00:29:57,838
Mr. Matlock, as,
uh, unusual as our...

546
00:29:57,862 --> 00:30:00,408
As our situation was here,

547
00:30:00,432 --> 00:30:03,512
I did not kill Marjorie.

548
00:30:03,536 --> 00:30:09,851
Of course... you were
mentioned heavily

549
00:30:09,875 --> 00:30:11,052
in her will, weren't you?

550
00:30:11,076 --> 00:30:12,454
I mean, everything.

551
00:30:12,478 --> 00:30:14,790
Yes, that's... That's correct.

552
00:30:14,814 --> 00:30:16,124
What if she had found out

553
00:30:16,148 --> 00:30:18,860
about you and, you
know, her sister?

554
00:30:18,884 --> 00:30:21,563
What if she had
decided to cut you out?

555
00:30:21,587 --> 00:30:23,498
And what if she didn't?

556
00:30:23,522 --> 00:30:25,700
Yeah.

557
00:30:25,724 --> 00:30:27,369
Yeah.

558
00:30:27,393 --> 00:30:29,192
You wouldn't have
any reason to kill her.

559
00:30:30,796 --> 00:30:34,842
Well, uh... Oh...
I'm... I'm sorry.

560
00:30:34,866 --> 00:30:37,634
It... It's a subpoena.

561
00:30:45,077 --> 00:30:46,676
Ms. Weston.

562
00:30:48,313 --> 00:30:49,880
Uh...

563
00:30:52,985 --> 00:30:55,918
I understand
congratulations are in order.

564
00:30:56,989 --> 00:30:58,333
For what?

565
00:30:58,357 --> 00:30:59,500
I hear the paper found someone

566
00:30:59,524 --> 00:31:01,604
to take over your
sister's column.

567
00:31:02,294 --> 00:31:04,039
That was supposed
to be a secret.

568
00:31:04,063 --> 00:31:05,440
Well...

569
00:31:05,464 --> 00:31:08,643
the editor in chief
of the<i> Examiner</i>

570
00:31:08,667 --> 00:31:10,145
is an old friend of mine,

571
00:31:10,169 --> 00:31:13,481
and he told me that
he was delighted

572
00:31:13,505 --> 00:31:15,884
when you approached
him with the idea.

573
00:31:15,908 --> 00:31:17,886
Well, it is kind of exciting.

574
00:31:17,910 --> 00:31:22,224
Well... now you have
your sister's column,

575
00:31:22,248 --> 00:31:26,661
her income, and...
maybe even her husband.

576
00:31:26,685 --> 00:31:28,796
I, uh...

577
00:31:28,820 --> 00:31:31,766
I wouldn't be doing
my job if I didn't ask you

578
00:31:31,790 --> 00:31:34,002
where you were at
the time of the murder.

579
00:31:34,026 --> 00:31:36,438
I was at home. Alone.

580
00:31:36,462 --> 00:31:38,306
Are you certain?

581
00:31:38,330 --> 00:31:40,075
Of course.

582
00:31:41,099 --> 00:31:42,265
Where was Lee?

583
00:31:43,502 --> 00:31:45,982
He said he was going
to a concert that night.

584
00:31:47,406 --> 00:31:48,817
Now, if you don't mind,

585
00:31:48,841 --> 00:31:51,274
you'll have to excuse
us, Mr. Matlock.

586
00:31:53,412 --> 00:31:56,257
Not only does this
conversation seem pointless,

587
00:31:56,281 --> 00:31:58,260
but we do have a
court in ten minutes.

588
00:31:58,284 --> 00:32:01,529
Oh, speaking of court.

589
00:32:01,553 --> 00:32:02,963
It's, uh...

590
00:32:02,987 --> 00:32:05,433
just a little subpoena
to appear in mine.

591
00:32:05,457 --> 00:32:07,323
Well, not mine, but, uh...

592
00:32:14,767 --> 00:32:16,811
Mr. Matlock. What
could be so urgent

593
00:32:16,835 --> 00:32:18,513
that it can't wait
until morning?

594
00:32:18,537 --> 00:32:21,249
My associate and
I asked each of you

595
00:32:21,273 --> 00:32:24,519
where you were the
night of the murder.

596
00:32:24,543 --> 00:32:27,221
And Carter, you said
you were home alone.

597
00:32:27,245 --> 00:32:29,057
Lee, you said you
were home alone.

598
00:32:29,081 --> 00:32:31,660
And Carol, you said
you were home alone.

599
00:32:31,684 --> 00:32:34,528
That's an awful lot of
people to be home<i> alone</i>

600
00:32:34,552 --> 00:32:36,064
at the time of this murder.

601
00:32:36,088 --> 00:32:38,899
Honey, you said you were
having dinner with Gladys.

602
00:32:38,923 --> 00:32:40,101
According to Gladys,

603
00:32:40,125 --> 00:32:42,537
she did not have
dinner with her.

604
00:32:42,561 --> 00:32:43,538
Where were you?

605
00:32:43,562 --> 00:32:45,407
I had a meeting.

606
00:32:45,431 --> 00:32:46,563
With whom?

607
00:32:47,967 --> 00:32:49,844
I had a meeting
with Carl Kravitz.

608
00:32:49,868 --> 00:32:52,714
His company has decided
to publish my novel.

609
00:32:52,738 --> 00:32:54,082
Novel?

610
00:32:54,106 --> 00:32:56,451
I've been writing a novel, yes.

611
00:32:56,475 --> 00:32:59,387
Well, why didn't you
say that to begin with?

612
00:32:59,411 --> 00:33:02,791
Because I have to publish
it with a pseudonym now.

613
00:33:02,815 --> 00:33:04,659
It's... It's a little explicit.

614
00:33:04,683 --> 00:33:08,296
Oh, you mean...
I mean, explicit.

615
00:33:08,320 --> 00:33:09,798
And I don't want
it to jeopardize

616
00:33:09,822 --> 00:33:11,733
my taking over
Marjorie's column.

617
00:33:11,757 --> 00:33:15,637
Uh, Ms. Weston, when...
When the copy boy got here

618
00:33:15,661 --> 00:33:17,438
at 10:00 that night

619
00:33:17,462 --> 00:33:19,707
and discovered
Marjorie Manners' body,

620
00:33:19,731 --> 00:33:23,633
he ran up to this house,
and no one was home.

621
00:33:25,003 --> 00:33:27,482
Now this, uh, Mr. Kravitz...

622
00:33:27,506 --> 00:33:29,105
how late were you with him?

623
00:33:30,642 --> 00:33:31,919
Well, I...

624
00:33:31,943 --> 00:33:36,591
I... I, uh, picked her
up from Kravitz' office.

625
00:33:36,615 --> 00:33:39,961
Wha...? What are
you talking about?

626
00:33:39,985 --> 00:33:42,864
I... Car... Carol was with me.

627
00:33:42,888 --> 00:33:43,864
Lee. LEE: No, it's...

628
00:33:43,888 --> 00:33:45,667
It's gonna come out anyway.

629
00:33:45,691 --> 00:33:51,027
Look, Carter, um...
you might as well know.

630
00:33:52,197 --> 00:33:54,642
Carol and I have...
Have been lovers.

631
00:33:54,666 --> 00:33:55,666
Y...?

632
00:33:57,169 --> 00:33:58,213
You...? Oh. No, you...!

633
00:33:58,237 --> 00:34:00,215
No. No, no, no. No. What?!

634
00:34:00,239 --> 00:34:02,717
No!

635
00:34:02,741 --> 00:34:06,954
Now, um... we know
that none of you

636
00:34:06,978 --> 00:34:09,790
were at home alone at
the time of the murder.

637
00:34:09,814 --> 00:34:12,293
And we know that none of you

638
00:34:12,317 --> 00:34:14,695
were where you told
each other you were.

639
00:34:14,719 --> 00:34:17,698
Now... Mr. Manners,

640
00:34:17,722 --> 00:34:20,734
according to my investigator,

641
00:34:20,758 --> 00:34:24,339
you were not at the
concert, as you said.

642
00:34:24,363 --> 00:34:25,640
Where were you...

643
00:34:25,664 --> 00:34:29,610
before you met Mrs. Weston?

644
00:34:29,634 --> 00:34:31,679
Very interesting
question, Mr. Matlock.

645
00:34:31,703 --> 00:34:35,249
Um... as a matter of
fact, I was in a meeting

646
00:34:35,273 --> 00:34:37,985
with the president of
Cendrex Chemicals.

647
00:34:38,009 --> 00:34:40,021
I was trying to get him

648
00:34:40,045 --> 00:34:43,358
placed as a head
of research in Detroit.

649
00:34:43,382 --> 00:34:45,760
I want him out of this
house, out of this city.

650
00:34:45,784 --> 00:34:47,751
I want him out of my life...

651
00:34:48,821 --> 00:34:52,867
so that I can have
Carol... for myself.

652
00:34:52,891 --> 00:34:55,070
You know, you're really
some kind of friend.

653
00:34:55,094 --> 00:34:56,938
Wait a minute. What about you?

654
00:34:56,962 --> 00:34:59,374
There's more than one liar here.

655
00:34:59,398 --> 00:35:00,909
You told me you were
going to the library

656
00:35:00,933 --> 00:35:02,710
the night Marjorie died.

657
00:35:02,734 --> 00:35:05,546
Why did you tell Mr. Matlock
that you were at home?

658
00:35:05,570 --> 00:35:07,449
Uh, no one saw
you at the library.

659
00:35:07,473 --> 00:35:08,605
We checked.

660
00:35:12,677 --> 00:35:14,717
All right, so I wasn't there.

661
00:35:16,447 --> 00:35:18,492
Well, where were you?

662
00:35:18,516 --> 00:35:19,916
What's the big secret?

663
00:35:21,686 --> 00:35:23,163
I was at a bar
near the university

664
00:35:23,187 --> 00:35:24,566
called the Cedar Lounge.

665
00:35:24,590 --> 00:35:25,967
The Cedar Lounge.

666
00:35:25,991 --> 00:35:28,403
That's a pickup bar for
young people, isn't it?

667
00:35:28,427 --> 00:35:29,404
That's right.

668
00:35:29,428 --> 00:35:32,473
That's exactly what it is.

669
00:35:32,497 --> 00:35:35,632
The reason I lied, Mr. Matlock,
is because I was embarrassed.

670
00:35:37,602 --> 00:35:39,280
And for your information...

671
00:35:39,304 --> 00:35:41,515
the reason I was at that
bar was because I was upset.

672
00:35:41,539 --> 00:35:43,739
And you wanna
know why I was upset?

673
00:35:45,677 --> 00:35:48,722
Because I was having
an affair with Marjorie...

674
00:35:48,746 --> 00:35:50,258
and she threw me
over for somebody else.

675
00:35:50,282 --> 00:35:51,492
Liar! No I'm not!

676
00:35:51,516 --> 00:35:52,993
No, no, no, no, no, no! Yes!

677
00:35:53,017 --> 00:35:54,596
This is working.
This is really working.

678
00:35:54,620 --> 00:35:56,631
This is wonderful.

679
00:35:56,655 --> 00:35:57,865
Now we know
where all of you were

680
00:35:57,889 --> 00:36:00,801
at the time of the murder,

681
00:36:00,825 --> 00:36:03,293
and I'm sure none of you did it.

682
00:36:07,899 --> 00:36:10,445
Who did Marjorie
throw you over for?

683
00:36:10,469 --> 00:36:12,213
Uh, excuse me.

684
00:36:12,237 --> 00:36:15,338
I just wanted to get some
hot water for some tea here.

685
00:36:22,514 --> 00:36:24,080
Sorry.

686
00:36:28,887 --> 00:36:29,952
Him.

687
00:36:32,390 --> 00:36:34,891
She threw me over for him.

688
00:36:42,534 --> 00:36:45,413
Mm. Marjorie Manners.

689
00:36:45,437 --> 00:36:47,782
Poof. Little did all
these people know

690
00:36:47,806 --> 00:36:50,017
that her life was just
one big pajama party.

691
00:36:50,041 --> 00:36:51,252
Yeah.

692
00:36:51,276 --> 00:36:52,987
Even Gideon was involved.

693
00:36:53,011 --> 00:36:55,190
At least he wasn't a
member of the family.

694
00:36:55,214 --> 00:36:56,691
Yeah.

695
00:36:56,715 --> 00:36:59,694
Now we know why Carter was
never able to find Marjorie's diary.

696
00:36:59,718 --> 00:37:01,729
Gideon must have found it first.

697
00:37:01,753 --> 00:37:03,898
Probably just
after he killed her.

698
00:37:03,922 --> 00:37:07,268
But he was at a seminar
from 9 to 11 that night.

699
00:37:07,292 --> 00:37:08,802
His alibi is airtight.

700
00:37:08,826 --> 00:37:13,173
If she were killed around
10. But what if, say...

701
00:37:13,197 --> 00:37:17,512
Gideon killed her...
a little before 9?

702
00:37:17,536 --> 00:37:20,481
The university is only
five minutes away.

703
00:37:20,505 --> 00:37:22,750
The time of death is
only an approximation.

704
00:37:22,774 --> 00:37:25,019
No, she couldn't
have been killed then.

705
00:37:25,043 --> 00:37:26,654
Her column.

706
00:37:26,678 --> 00:37:29,890
It was just one sentence
away from being done.

707
00:37:29,914 --> 00:37:32,527
If she was killed at 9, when
could she have written it?

708
00:37:32,551 --> 00:37:33,961
How about before that?

709
00:37:33,985 --> 00:37:37,065
No. The people she was at
dinner with dropped her off at 8:50.

710
00:37:37,089 --> 00:37:41,169
Well, that column had to
have been written before 9.

711
00:37:41,193 --> 00:37:42,736
That's all there is to it.

712
00:37:42,760 --> 00:37:44,138
Well, it's impossible.

713
00:37:44,162 --> 00:37:45,873
She couldn't have done it.

714
00:37:45,897 --> 00:37:49,766
What kind of typewriter
does Gideon have?

715
00:37:51,703 --> 00:37:53,903
One just like this.

716
00:37:56,374 --> 00:37:58,107
Well, that's interesting.

717
00:38:01,346 --> 00:38:03,091
I had an Underwood...

718
00:38:03,115 --> 00:38:05,259
all the way through law school.

719
00:38:05,283 --> 00:38:09,097
I remember the way it smelled...

720
00:38:09,121 --> 00:38:12,355
the way the little bell
sounded on the carriage return.

721
00:38:14,092 --> 00:38:17,727
And how it ruined my day
when I had to change the ribbon.

722
00:38:20,431 --> 00:38:23,611
It always turned
out to be a mess.

723
00:38:25,303 --> 00:38:30,018
But with the cartridges
they have nowadays,

724
00:38:30,042 --> 00:38:31,686
you don't even need
to look at the ribbon,

725
00:38:31,710 --> 00:38:33,509
let alone touch it.

726
00:38:38,816 --> 00:38:41,962
Um... Mr. Harris...

727
00:38:41,986 --> 00:38:46,434
you live on the Manners' estate?

728
00:38:46,458 --> 00:38:48,102
Yes, I do.

729
00:38:48,126 --> 00:38:51,506
Uh, upstairs in a room
in the main house?

730
00:38:51,530 --> 00:38:53,074
That's right.

731
00:38:53,098 --> 00:38:55,343
How would you
describe your relationship

732
00:38:55,367 --> 00:38:58,001
with Marjorie Manners?

733
00:39:00,538 --> 00:39:01,818
We were friends.

734
00:39:04,242 --> 00:39:06,019
Did you ever have
an affair with her?

735
00:39:06,043 --> 00:39:07,689
Never.

736
00:39:07,713 --> 00:39:11,859
So you're just a
struggling Ph.D. candidate,

737
00:39:11,883 --> 00:39:14,161
uh, seeking a
convenient place to live

738
00:39:14,185 --> 00:39:15,996
while he writes
out his dissertation.

739
00:39:16,020 --> 00:39:17,764
Is that about
right? That's right.

740
00:39:17,788 --> 00:39:19,400
Uh, do you mind
telling the court

741
00:39:19,424 --> 00:39:20,935
uh, what your
dissertation was about?

742
00:39:20,959 --> 00:39:22,403
Objection. Relevancy.

743
00:39:22,427 --> 00:39:24,571
I assume there's a
point to this, Mr. Matlock.

744
00:39:24,595 --> 00:39:26,140
Absolutely, Your Honor.

745
00:39:26,164 --> 00:39:27,508
Overruled.

746
00:39:27,532 --> 00:39:30,243
Answer the question, please.

747
00:39:30,267 --> 00:39:31,779
My dissertation is a study

748
00:39:31,803 --> 00:39:34,648
of the sociopsychological
ramifications

749
00:39:34,672 --> 00:39:38,319
of the print-media phenomenon
known as advice columns.

750
00:39:38,343 --> 00:39:40,121
Specifically?

751
00:39:40,145 --> 00:39:42,757
The major portion
of my dissertation

752
00:39:42,781 --> 00:39:44,859
is a study of "Dear Marjorie."

753
00:39:44,883 --> 00:39:46,293
Marjorie Manners' column.

754
00:39:46,317 --> 00:39:47,929
That's correct.

755
00:39:47,953 --> 00:39:51,866
So you... You analyze her column

756
00:39:51,890 --> 00:39:56,170
and the effect it has on
the people who follow it.

757
00:39:56,194 --> 00:39:57,705
Yes.

758
00:39:57,729 --> 00:40:00,974
Is it a serious, scientific,
objective analysis?

759
00:40:00,998 --> 00:40:03,844
Definitely.

760
00:40:03,868 --> 00:40:06,247
What would
happen to the validity

761
00:40:06,271 --> 00:40:11,318
of such a dissertation
if it were known

762
00:40:11,342 --> 00:40:14,155
that the author
of that dissertation

763
00:40:14,179 --> 00:40:16,891
were sleeping with the
creator of the subject?

764
00:40:16,915 --> 00:40:20,595
I'm speaking of Marjorie
Manners of "Dear Marjorie."

765
00:40:21,819 --> 00:40:23,998
The integrity of the
work would be suspect.

766
00:40:24,022 --> 00:40:26,400
The integrity of the work would
be out the window, wouldn't it?

767
00:40:26,424 --> 00:40:27,634
Well... The Ph.D. would be

768
00:40:27,658 --> 00:40:28,869
out the window, wouldn't it?

769
00:40:28,893 --> 00:40:30,637
And the years you spent
working for that Ph.D.

770
00:40:30,661 --> 00:40:31,939
would be out the window, right?

771
00:40:31,963 --> 00:40:33,541
Objection.

772
00:40:33,565 --> 00:40:35,542
Mr. Matlock. I'll move
on to another line

773
00:40:35,566 --> 00:40:36,699
of questions, Your Honor.

774
00:40:40,238 --> 00:40:42,805
Ahem. Mr. Harris, uh...

775
00:40:45,610 --> 00:40:48,722
did you know that, uh...

776
00:40:48,746 --> 00:40:51,625
Marjorie Manners kept a diary?

777
00:40:51,649 --> 00:40:53,593
Yes.

778
00:40:53,617 --> 00:40:55,229
You know it's missing?

779
00:40:55,253 --> 00:40:56,764
No, I-I didn't know.

780
00:40:56,788 --> 00:40:58,588
Yeah. Yeah.

781
00:41:00,759 --> 00:41:03,971
What was in it, Mr. Harris?

782
00:41:03,995 --> 00:41:07,062
Was she giving you too
much unsolicited advice?

783
00:41:08,432 --> 00:41:11,478
Was she asking more than
you were prepared to give?

784
00:41:11,502 --> 00:41:13,881
Were you trying to get
out of the relationship?

785
00:41:13,905 --> 00:41:15,850
Was she threatening
to go public with it?

786
00:41:15,874 --> 00:41:17,384
Is that why you killed her?

787
00:41:17,408 --> 00:41:18,385
Objection, Your Honor.

788
00:41:18,409 --> 00:41:19,486
No foundation.

789
00:41:19,510 --> 00:41:20,721
Speculative, argumentative.

790
00:41:20,745 --> 00:41:22,912
Withdraw. Withdraw the question.

791
00:41:23,982 --> 00:41:27,761
Um, Mr. Harris, uh...

792
00:41:27,785 --> 00:41:31,231
on the day that, uh...

793
00:41:31,255 --> 00:41:33,000
Marjorie Manners was killed,

794
00:41:33,024 --> 00:41:37,237
did you hear her say that
in celebration of her award

795
00:41:37,261 --> 00:41:38,371
she was going out to dinner

796
00:41:38,395 --> 00:41:41,208
with some very old
and dear friends,

797
00:41:41,232 --> 00:41:44,144
and her column would be finished

798
00:41:44,168 --> 00:41:45,980
at 10:00 instead of 6?

799
00:41:46,004 --> 00:41:48,749
I think everybody at
the party heard that.

800
00:41:48,773 --> 00:41:52,586
And you knew that
Harold, the copy boy,

801
00:41:52,610 --> 00:41:54,843
would come by at 10:00?

802
00:41:56,180 --> 00:41:57,758
Again, everybody did.

803
00:41:57,782 --> 00:41:59,126
Just answer yes or no.

804
00:41:59,150 --> 00:42:00,561
Yes.

805
00:42:00,585 --> 00:42:05,533
And you heard
her tell my client...

806
00:42:05,557 --> 00:42:07,835
to come by her office at 10:00?

807
00:42:07,859 --> 00:42:09,303
Yes.

808
00:42:09,327 --> 00:42:13,841
While you were at the party,
did you see my client pick this up?

809
00:42:13,865 --> 00:42:15,176
Yes.

810
00:42:15,200 --> 00:42:18,446
Uh, you knew his
fingerprints were on it?

811
00:42:18,470 --> 00:42:19,447
Yes.

812
00:42:19,471 --> 00:42:22,183
Mr. Harris, isn't it
true that you went

813
00:42:22,207 --> 00:42:25,820
to Marjorie Manners' office
that night, and, wearing gloves,

814
00:42:25,844 --> 00:42:28,889
used this to deliver the
fatal blow to her head?

815
00:42:28,913 --> 00:42:30,357
Objection, Your Honor.

816
00:42:30,381 --> 00:42:33,394
Again, no foundation,
speculative and argumentative.

817
00:42:33,418 --> 00:42:35,618
Withdraw. Withdraw.

818
00:42:42,494 --> 00:42:47,864
You did... go to
her office that night.

819
00:42:48,933 --> 00:42:50,366
Didn't you, Mr. Harris?

820
00:42:51,635 --> 00:42:53,713
Mr. Matlock, I'm
sorry to disappoint you,

821
00:42:53,737 --> 00:42:56,317
but I was nowhere
near her office that night.

822
00:42:56,341 --> 00:42:57,684
I was in a seminar.

823
00:42:57,708 --> 00:43:00,487
Yeah. After 9.

824
00:43:00,511 --> 00:43:01,710
I know that.

825
00:43:03,648 --> 00:43:06,193
And I know that her column

826
00:43:06,217 --> 00:43:07,694
was found in her typewriter,

827
00:43:07,718 --> 00:43:09,163
indicating she had
been working on it

828
00:43:09,187 --> 00:43:12,533
for... an hour.

829
00:43:12,557 --> 00:43:14,001
But what if she didn't write it?

830
00:43:14,025 --> 00:43:16,437
What if it were
written by someone

831
00:43:16,461 --> 00:43:20,040
who knew that
column intimately well?

832
00:43:20,064 --> 00:43:21,742
I don't understand.

833
00:43:21,766 --> 00:43:23,076
You wrote the
column, didn't you?

834
00:43:23,100 --> 00:43:24,478
Up in your room,
on your typewriter,

835
00:43:24,502 --> 00:43:25,946
while she was at dinner. No.

836
00:43:25,970 --> 00:43:27,715
Then you put it
in her typewriter.

837
00:43:27,739 --> 00:43:29,617
You went by her office.

838
00:43:29,641 --> 00:43:33,120
You killed her shortly before 9,

839
00:43:33,144 --> 00:43:34,988
and left that column...

840
00:43:35,012 --> 00:43:37,425
as proof that she
was killed around 10.

841
00:43:37,449 --> 00:43:39,192
No. Absolutely not.

842
00:43:39,216 --> 00:43:40,761
I'd like this typewriter

843
00:43:40,785 --> 00:43:44,754
introduced as Defense Exhibit C.

844
00:43:46,156 --> 00:43:47,823
Do you recognize
this typewriter?

845
00:43:49,527 --> 00:43:50,504
It looks like mine.

846
00:43:50,528 --> 00:43:52,462
It is yours.

847
00:43:53,698 --> 00:43:55,765
Would you remove the ribbon?

848
00:44:04,943 --> 00:44:06,821
There you go.

849
00:44:06,845 --> 00:44:11,125
Now, what if we had
the Forensics department

850
00:44:11,149 --> 00:44:12,693
of the Atlanta police

851
00:44:12,717 --> 00:44:16,463
examine the impressions

852
00:44:16,487 --> 00:44:18,699
on this typewriter ribbon.

853
00:44:18,723 --> 00:44:20,534
Transcribe it, so to speak.

854
00:44:20,558 --> 00:44:22,758
What would they find?

855
00:44:24,762 --> 00:44:30,077
A copy of my
dissertation, some letters.

856
00:44:30,101 --> 00:44:32,146
That's right.

857
00:44:33,938 --> 00:44:35,038
What else?

858
00:44:40,378 --> 00:44:42,522
"Dear Marjorie's" last column.

859
00:44:45,149 --> 00:44:49,330
"Dear Angry, Tense, Unhappy:

860
00:44:49,354 --> 00:44:50,931
"My advice to you

861
00:44:50,955 --> 00:44:53,533
"is stay with your husband.

862
00:44:53,557 --> 00:44:56,937
"I know that most of
the time he is either

863
00:44:56,961 --> 00:45:00,874
"too tired, or his back hurts,

864
00:45:00,898 --> 00:45:03,944
"but there is that
once in a while.

865
00:45:03,968 --> 00:45:05,446
"Keep a good thought,

866
00:45:05,470 --> 00:45:07,548
and make the best of
that once in a while."

867
00:45:08,973 --> 00:45:10,139
That's good advice.

868
00:45:11,542 --> 00:45:14,755
You know what I
think, Mr. Harris?

869
00:45:14,779 --> 00:45:18,759
I think you would have
made a wonderful replacement

870
00:45:18,783 --> 00:45:21,717
for Marjorie Manners...

871
00:45:24,255 --> 00:45:25,687
if you hadn't killed her.

872
00:45:42,673 --> 00:45:44,518
Anna...

873
00:45:44,542 --> 00:45:47,421
there's something
I have to confess.

874
00:45:47,445 --> 00:45:50,191
All those women you
thought I was with?

875
00:45:50,215 --> 00:45:51,891
Well, you were right.

876
00:45:51,915 --> 00:45:53,127
Uh, they couldn't resist me,

877
00:45:53,151 --> 00:45:55,162
and I couldn't
resist the temptation.

878
00:45:55,186 --> 00:45:57,264
I knew it. Oh, but I'm never,

879
00:45:57,288 --> 00:45:59,700
ever going to do
anything like that again.

880
00:45:59,724 --> 00:46:01,134
I'm going to be strong.

881
00:46:01,158 --> 00:46:02,969
I promise.

882
00:46:02,993 --> 00:46:04,338
Promise? CRAIG: Mm.

883
00:46:04,362 --> 00:46:06,941
Going without you has
taught me a big lesson.

884
00:46:06,965 --> 00:46:09,476
I love you and only you.

885
00:46:09,500 --> 00:46:12,201
You don't have to
worry about me anymore.

886
00:46:19,410 --> 00:46:21,377
Let's go home.

887
00:46:27,018 --> 00:46:30,831
Aw. I'm really happy for them.

888
00:46:30,855 --> 00:46:32,021
Yeah.

889
00:46:33,190 --> 00:46:34,190
Yeah.

890
00:46:38,229 --> 00:46:41,976
Oh, how are things
with, uh... Brad?

891
00:46:42,000 --> 00:46:43,398
I took your advice.

892
00:46:44,668 --> 00:46:46,113
Yeah? Yeah.

893
00:46:46,137 --> 00:46:47,414
I told him I wanted to go,

894
00:46:47,438 --> 00:46:49,838
but I wanted to
have my own room.

895
00:46:50,708 --> 00:46:51,708
Yeah?

896
00:46:52,710 --> 00:46:54,655
He already booked me one.

897
00:46:54,679 --> 00:46:56,390
Separate rooms? Yeah.

898
00:46:56,414 --> 00:46:57,734
Oh.

899
00:46:58,683 --> 00:46:59,683
Ha, ha.

900
00:47:00,852 --> 00:47:03,196
I think he actually likes me.

901
00:47:03,220 --> 00:47:04,965
Oh, well.

902
00:47:04,989 --> 00:47:06,989
Give yourself some credit.

903
00:47:08,326 --> 00:47:09,470
Thanks.

9999
00:00:0,500 --> 00:00:2,00
